2026-06-30 15:51:24,022 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-fffee7da-137b-424d-a2b9-bb9886105d79', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms related to sample preparation and digestion.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: cleavage agent details\nsdrf_column: comment[cleavage agent details]\nontology: MS:1001044 (PSI-MS)\ndescription: Enzyme or chemical used for protein digestion.\ninstructions: Use PSI-MS ontology terms. Multiple enzymes are common in XL-MS.\n  If multiple, return a list.\n  Common terms:\n    Trypsin (MS:1001251) — cleaves K,R (not before P)\n    Lys-C (MS:1001309) — cleaves K\n    Lys-C/P (MS:1001309) — cleaves K including before P\n    Asp-N (MS:1001303) — cleaves N-terminal to D\n    Glu-C (MS:1001917) — cleaves C-terminal to E, D\n    Chymotrypsin (MS:1001306) — cleaves F,Y,W,L\n    No cleavage (MS:1001955)\n    Unspecific cleavage (MS:1001956)\n  Format each as: NT=<enzyme name>;AC=<MS accession>\n\nterm: label\nsdrf_column: comment[label]\nontology: PRIDE:0000514, MS\ndescription: Labeling strategy used for quantification.\ninstructions: Most XL-MS is label-free. Use:\n  NT=label free sample;AC=MS:1002038 — for label-free\n  NT=SILAC;AC=MS:1002038 — for SILAC\n  NT=iTRAQ4plex;AC=PRIDE:0000114 — for iTRAQ 4-plex\n  NT=TMT6plex;AC=PRIDE:0000116 — for TMT\n  If not stated, assume label free.\n\nterm: alkylation reagent\nsdrf_column: comment[alkylation reagent]\nontology: PRIDE:0000598\ndescription: Chemical reagent used for cysteine alkylation (typically before digestion).\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Common: iodoacetamide (IAA), iodoacetic acid,\n  N-ethylmaleimide (NEM), acrylamide. If iodoacetamide, use "iodoacetamide".\n\nterm: fractionation method\nsdrf_column: comment[fractionation method]\nontology: PRIDE:0000550\ndescription: Peptide fractionation method used before LC-MS/MS (distinct from crosslink\n  enrichment — this is standard proteomics fractionation).\ninstructions: Return null if no pre-LC fractionation. Examples: "high-pH reversed-phase\n  chromatography", "basic reversed-phase LC", "SCX fractionation", "HILIC".\n\nterm: modification parameters\nsdrf_column: comment[modification parameters]\nontology: MS:1001055, Unimod, PSI-MOD\ndescription: Post-translational modifications searched in the database search.\noutput_format: List of modifications, each formatted as:\n  NT=<mod name>;AC=<Unimod accession>;TA=<target amino acid>;MT=<Fixed or Variable>\ninstructions: Always include carbamidomethyl cysteine if IAA alkylation was used:\n  NT=Carbamidomethyl;AC=UNIMOD:4;TA=C;MT=Fixed\n  Always include oxidation of methionine if searched:\n  NT=Oxidation;AC=UNIMOD:21;TA=M;MT=Variable\n  Include crosslinker mono-link (dead-end) modifications if mentioned.\n  Return a list even if only one modification.\n\nterm: fraction identifier\nsdrf_column: comment[fraction identifier]\nontology: MS:1000858\ndescription: Fraction number for fractionated samples.\ninstructions: Describe whether the dataset uses fractionation and what the fractionation\n  scheme is (e.g. "12 SEC fractions per replicate", "no fractionation").\n  The actual integer values are assigned per-file; describe the scheme here.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null\n\nFor comment[modification parameters], "value" should be a list of formatted strings.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nIdentification of TDP-43 - TAU interactions by XL-MS\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nIdentification of TDP-43 - TAU interactions by XL-MS (DSS). The interactions were identified in for equimolar amount of TDP-43 - TAU and, as control, TDP-43 and MBP. After 30 minutes from the induction of phase separation, samples were either analyzed (bulk condition) or subjected to centrifugation (10min at 21’000g) to pellet the formed condensates.\n\n[METHODS]\nFor XL-MS analysis, three independent experiments were conducted to determine whether TDP-43 can physically interact with TAU and to characterize the topology of this interaction. 10\u202fµM TDP-43-TEV-MBP-His₆ was cleaved with 0.08\u202fmg/ml His₆-TEV protease in the presence of either 10\u202fµM MBP (control) or 10\u202fµM TAU441 in 50\u202fµl of reaction buffer (20\u202fmM HEPES, 150\u202fmM NaCl, 1\u202fmM DTT, pH 7.5). After 30 minutes from the induction of phase separation, samples were either analyzed (bulk condition) or subjected to centrifugation (10min at 21’000g) to pellet the formed condensates. For the pellet condition, the resulting pellet was resuspended in 50µl reaction buffer. Both bulk and pellet samples from TDP-43 incubated with MBP or TAU were subjected to crosslinking reaction with 1mM isotope labeled di-succimidylsuberate (DSS-d0 and DSS-d12, Creative Molecules Inc.). After 5 minutes of reaction, the samples were subjected to liquid nitrogen quenching and thawed in 50mM Ammonium bicarbonate. Upon quenching, samples were dried, dissolved in 8\u2009M Urea, reduced (5\u2009mM TCEP), and alkylated (10\u2009mM iodoacetamide). The proteolysis was performed overnight at 37\u2009°C in 1\u2009M urea with 1µg trypsin (Promega, Sequencing grade). After quenching (5% formic acid) peptides were subjected to cleanup according to manufacture procedures (AttractSPE Tips, Affinisep). Data acquisition: Liquid chromatography-tandem mass spectrometry (LC-MS/MS) measurements were performed on Orbitrap QExactive+ MS coupled to a Dionex Ultimate 3000 RSLCnano System liquid chromatography system. Peptides were loaded on a commercial trap column (μ-Precolumn C18 PepMap100, C18, 300\u2009μm I.D., 5\u2009μm particle size) and separated using a reverse phase column Thermo PepMap100 C18 (50\u2009cm length, 75\u2009μm inner diameter, 35\u2009μm particle size). For the identification of crosslinking peptides in DDA mode, an LC method was set up to separate peptides across a gradient from 8%B to 25%B in 60 min and from 25%B to 50%B in 4\u2009min (buffer A: 0.1% (v/v) formic acid; buffer B: 0.1% (v/v) formic acid, 80% (v/v) acetonitrile). The acquisition method was performed with one MS1 scan followed by a maximum of 20 scans for the top 20 most intense peptides (TOP20) with MS1 scans (R\u2009=\u200970,000 at 400\u2009m/z, maxIT\u2009=\u2009100\u2009ms AGC=3e6), HCD fragmentation (NCE\u2009=\u200930%), isolation windows (1.6m/z) and MS2 scans (R\u2009=\u200917,500 at 400\u2009m/z, maxIT\u2009=\u200950\u2009ms, AGC\u2009=\u20091e5). A dynamic exclusion of 30\u2009s was applied, and charge states lower than three and higher than seven were rejected for the isolation.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
