(K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:09317), DSPU (K,nterm), DSSBU (K,nterm),\n    DHSO (carbonyl-reactive D,E; AC=XLMOD:02226), BMSO (cysteine-specific; C),\n    SDASO (K,nterm; photo+MS-cleavable), SDASO-L (K,nterm; photo+MS-cleavable),\n    SSD (K,nterm), CBDPS (K,nterm), iQlnk (K,nterm)\n  PIR-type enrichable (reporter-ion, NOT backbone MS-cleavable): PIR (K,nterm), BDP-NHP (K,nterm)\n  Common non-cleavable: BS3/BS2G (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02000/02005), DSS (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02001), EDC (D,E,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02010), formaldehyde (K,nterm)\n  IMAC-enrichable (non-cleavable): PhoX (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02258), pDSPE (K,nterm; AC=null)\n  Photo-crosslinkers (non-cleavable, UV-activated): diazirine (K or incorporated aa), benzophenone (Bpa, pBpa; C or incorporated aa), SDA/sulfo-SDA (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02134/02136), aryl azide\n  NOTE: Do NOT extract proximity labeling enzymes (TurboID, BioID, APEX, biotin-phenol+H2O2) as crosslinkers — these are NOT chemical cross-linking reagents.\n\nterm: crosslinker concentration\nsdrf_column: comment[crosslinker concentration]\nontology: PRIDE:0000951\ndescription: Concentration of the crosslinking reagent used in the reaction.\npattern: number + mM or uM (e.g. "1 mM", "500 uM", "0.5 mM")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: crosslinker to protein ratio\nsdrf_column: comment[crosslinker to protein ratio]\nontology: PRIDE:0000952\ndescription: Molar ratio of crosslinker to protein in the reaction.\npattern: ratio expression (e.g. "50:1", "100-fold molar excess", "5 mM per 1 mg/mL")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: quenching reagent\nsdrf_column: comment[quenching reagent]\nontology: PRIDE:0000953\ndescription: Reagent used to stop, quench, neutralize, terminate, or consume the crosslinking reaction.\ninstructions:\n  Extract only if explicitly stated in the paper, PRIDE metadata, or cited protocol text.\n  Search for synonyms including: quenched, stopped, terminated, neutralized, blocked,\n  residual crosslinker, washed with, incubated with, reaction was halted.\nCommon quenchers:\n  - NHS ester crosslinkers such as BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU: Tris, ammonium bicarbonate, glycine, lysine\n  - Formaldehyde crosslinking: glycine or Tris are common, but only annotate if explicitly stated\nFor formaldehyde datasets:\n  - Inspect the 3–5 sentences after the formaldehyde crosslinking sentence.\n  - If the text says "as described previously" or "as described before", and no quencher is named, attempt to read the cited artical and extract the quenching reagent from its methods section.\n    If that fall back failes then return value=null with confidence="low" and reasoning="quenching reagent not explicitly stated in provided text; may require cited protocol."\n  - Do NOT infer glycine solely because formaldehyde was used.\n  - Do NOT treat PBS washes alone as a quenching reagent.\n  - Treat Tris-containing lysis buffer as a possible quencher only if the text clearly implies it was use\n  \nterm: crosslink distance\nsdrf_column: characteristics[crosslink distance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000948\ndescription: Maximum Cα–Cα distance constraint (spacer arm length) provided by the crosslinker.\npattern: number + Å (e.g. "11.4 Å", "30 Å", "~27 Å")\ninstructions: Extract the stated spacer length or max distance. Common values:\n  BS3/DSS ≈ 11.4 Å, DSSO ≈ 10.1 Å, DSBU ≈ 12.5 Å, EDC ≈ 0 Å (zero-length).\n  Return null if not explicitly stated in the text.\n\nterm: chemical cross-linking coupled with ms\nsdrf_column: comment[chemical cross-linking coupled with ms]\nontology: PRIDE:0000430\ndescription: MS-based cross-linking methodology identifier.\nallowed_values: cross-linking mass spectrometry\ninstructions: Always return "cross-linking mass spectrometry" for any XL-MS dataset.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null). For comment[cross-linker] the\n           "value" field should contain the full NT=...;AC=...;CL=...;TA=... string,\n           and also include separate sub-fields: "NT", "AC", "CL", "TA".\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null\n\nExample for comment[cross-linker]:\n{\n  "comment[cross-linker]": {\n    "value": "NT=DSSO;AC=XLMOD:02056;CL=yes;TA=K,S,T,Y",\n    "NT": "DSSO", "AC": "XLMOD:02056", "CL": "yes", "TA": "K,S,T,Y",\n    "confidence": "high", "evidence_quote": "crosslinked with 1 mM DSSO",\n    "evidence_location": "Methods"\n  }\n}'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nIdentification of TDP-43 - TAU interactions by XL-MS\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nIdentification of TDP-43 - TAU interactions by XL-MS (DSS). The interactions were identified in for equimolar amount of TDP-43 - TAU and, as control, TDP-43 and MBP. After 30 minutes from the induction of phase separation, samples were either analyzed (bulk condition) or subjected to centrifugation (10min at 21’000g) to pellet the formed condensates.\n\n[METHODS]\nFor XL-MS analysis, three independent experiments were conducted to determine whether TDP-43 can physically interact with TAU and to characterize the topology of this interaction. 10\u202fµM TDP-43-TEV-MBP-His₆ was cleaved with 0.08\u202fmg/ml His₆-TEV protease in the presence of either 10\u202fµM MBP (control) or 10\u202fµM TAU441 in 50\u202fµl of reaction buffer (20\u202fmM HEPES, 150\u202fmM NaCl, 1\u202fmM DTT, pH 7.5). After 30 minutes from the induction of phase separation, samples were either analyzed (bulk condition) or subjected to centrifugation (10min at 21’000g) to pellet the formed condensates. For the pellet condition, the resulting pellet was resuspended in 50µl reaction buffer. Both bulk and pellet samples from TDP-43 incubated with MBP or TAU were subjected to crosslinking reaction with 1mM isotope labeled di-succimidylsuberate (DSS-d0 and DSS-d12, Creative Molecules Inc.). After 5 minutes of reaction, the samples were subjected to liquid nitrogen quenching and thawed in 50mM Ammonium bicarbonate. Upon quenching, samples were dried, dissolved in 8\u2009M Urea, reduced (5\u2009mM TCEP), and alkylated (10\u2009mM iodoacetamide). The proteolysis was performed overnight at 37\u2009°C in 1\u2009M urea with 1µg trypsin (Promega, Sequencing grade). After quenching (5% formic acid) peptides were subjected to cleanup according to manufacture procedures (AttractSPE Tips, Affinisep). Data acquisition: Liquid chromatography-tandem mass spectrometry (LC-MS/MS) measurements were performed on Orbitrap QExactive+ MS coupled to a Dionex Ultimate 3000 RSLCnano System liquid chromatography system. Peptides were loaded on a commercial trap column (μ-Precolumn C18 PepMap100, C18, 300\u2009μm I.D., 5\u2009μm particle size) and separated using a reverse phase column Thermo PepMap100 C18 (50\u2009cm length, 75\u2009μm inner diameter, 35\u2009μm particle size). For the identification of crosslinking peptides in DDA mode, an LC method was set up to separate peptides across a gradient from 8%B to 25%B in 60 min and from 25%B to 50%B in 4\u2009min (buffer A: 0.1% (v/v) formic acid; buffer B: 0.1% (v/v) formic acid, 80% (v/v) acetonitrile). The acquisition method was performed with one MS1 scan followed by a maximum of 20 scans for the top 20 most intense peptides (TOP20) with MS1 scans (R\u2009=\u200970,000 at 400\u2009m/z, maxIT\u2009=\u2009100\u2009ms AGC=3e6), HCD fragmentation (NCE\u2009=\u200930%), isolation windows (1.6m/z) and MS2 scans (R\u2009=\u200917,500 at 400\u2009m/z, maxIT\u2009=\u200950\u2009ms, AGC\u2009=\u20091e5). A dynamic exclusion of 30\u2009s was applied, and charge states lower than three and higher than seven were rejected for the isolation.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'sto